WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.800
The film's tree and your simulation both gave each kind its own zone by rule.

2
00:00:06.180 --> 00:00:09.040
Did real warblers use spruce trees in different patterns?

3
00:00:10.380 --> 00:00:15.120
To find out, keep the same tree and replace imagined paths with recorded

4
00:00:15.120 --> 00:00:16.120
observations.

5
00:00:19.483 --> 00:00:23.343
Robert MacArthur divided spruce trees into named zones.

6
00:00:24.823 --> 00:00:26.563
Height bands ran down from the treetop.

7
00:00:27.863 --> 00:00:31.083
Each branch also had a base, a middle, and outer tips.

8
00:00:32.403 --> 00:00:35.823
Using the same map for every bird made their patterns comparable.

9
00:00:36.983 --> 00:00:41.103
When MacArthur could see a bird, he timed how long it stayed in each zone.

10
00:00:42.403 --> 00:00:45.063
The forest was dense, and birds were often hidden.

11
00:00:46.483 --> 00:00:49.303
Many hours of watching produced few useful seconds.

12
00:00:50.603 --> 00:00:54.123
Each colored timer fills only while its bird remains visible.

13
00:00:55.233 --> 00:01:00.533
For each kind, the recorded seconds became percentages of its total recorded time.

14
00:01:02.013 --> 00:01:03.573
Darker zones mean more time there.

15
00:01:04.773 --> 00:01:09.493
The maps show different centers of activity, but their colored areas still overlap.

16
00:01:10.793 --> 00:01:13.073
No bird owns a private slice of the tree.

17
00:01:14.353 --> 00:01:16.913
Some kinds centered activity high in the tree.

18
00:01:18.253 --> 00:01:21.153
Others centered lower, or spread their time more widely.

19
00:01:21.153 --> 00:01:22.153
. . . .

20
00:01:22.267 --> 00:01:24.147
Predict how to read a shared zone.

21
00:01:25.527 --> 00:01:27.787
Both kinds clearly have recorded time there.

22
00:01:29.067 --> 00:01:32.367
Does that show complete separation, or does it show overlap?

23
00:01:33.707 --> 00:01:35.627
Hold your answer while both cells glow.

24
00:01:41.633 --> 00:01:46.553
It shows overlap. Both kinds spent recorded time in that zone.

25
00:01:47.813 --> 00:01:52.353
Their percentages can still differ, so overlap does not mean identical use.

26
00:01:53.713 --> 00:01:58.613
Read down a column to compare kinds, then read across a row to find each kind's

27
00:01:58.613 --> 00:02:03.133
pattern. A large value marks a strong center of activity.

28
00:02:04.573 --> 00:02:07.533
Smaller values still matter because they show shared use.

29
00:02:08.583 --> 00:02:11.223
Now separate observation from explanation.

30
00:02:12.763 --> 00:02:15.103
The table records time, not insects eaten.

31
00:02:16.363 --> 00:02:19.283
Some records were singing birds, not feeding birds.

32
00:02:20.603 --> 00:02:23.983
Visibility matters because dense branches can hide birds.

33
00:02:25.443 --> 00:02:30.203
Percentages summarize what the observer could record, not everything birds did.

34
00:02:31.223 --> 00:02:34.183
The study did not directly measure competition.

35
00:02:35.483 --> 00:02:40.603
Different tree use may reduce competition, but the table alone cannot prove that

36
00:02:40.603 --> 00:02:41.603
cause.

37
00:02:42.117 --> 00:02:44.537
A careful claim keeps those parts apart.

38
00:02:45.797 --> 00:02:50.237
The warblers shared a habitat and used it in different, overlapping patterns.

39
00:02:51.537 --> 00:02:55.457
That pattern carefully supports a possible explanation for sharing.

40
00:02:56.657 --> 00:03:00.257
It remains evidence for the idea, not proof of every step.
